{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0092","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How did the focus of the 2016 NIST Speaker Recognition Evaluation differ from previous years?","answer":"The 2016 evaluation marked a major shift from English towards Austronesian and Chinese languages, emphasizing speaker recognition technology that is robust to novel, heterogeneous languages with less training data."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0092","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What speaker recognition approach does the Intelligent Voice (IV) system use?","answer":"It is based on the state-of-the-art i-vector/PLDA approach, developed on the fixed training condition."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0092","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How many unlabelled telephone calls did NIST provide for minor and major languages, and which languages were they?","answer":"NIST provided 2472 unlabelled telephone calls from minor languages (Cebuano and Mandarin) and major languages (Tagalog and Cantonese)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0092","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What frame-level miss-classification rate did the DNN-HMM speech activity detector achieve compared to an energy-based SAD?","answer":"The DNN-HMM SAD achieved a 5.9% frame-level miss-classification rate on 50 hours of telephone speech data, while an energy-based SAD did not perform better than 20%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0092","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is the architecture of the DNN-HMM speech activity detector used in the IV system?","answer":"It is a two-class DNN-HMM classifier with 6 hidden layers of 512 units each, taking 40-dimensional filter-bank features plus 7 frames from each side as input."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0092","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What acoustic features and frame lengths did the Intelligent Voice system use?","answer":"It used 39-dimensional PLP features (25ms frames) and 60-dimensional MFCC features (20ms frames), both with a 10ms overlap, extracted at 8kHz using Kaldi."}
